        '**CELL STRUCTURE — EUKARYOTIC vs PROKARYOTIC**\n\nAll living things are made of cells. There are two fundamental types:\n\n**EUKARYOTIC CELLS** (animals, plants, fungi) — have a TRUE nucleus containing DNA enclosed by a nuclear membrane. They are typically 10-100 μm in size.\n\n**Animal cells** contain:\n• Nucleus — contains DNA (genetic information), controls cell activities\n• Cytoplasm — jelly-like fluid where most chemical reactions occur\n• Cell membrane — controls what enters and exits (selectively permeable)\n• Mitochondria — site of aerobic respiration, produces ATP (energy)\n• Ribosomes — site of protein synthesis (found in cytoplasm and on rough ER)\n\n**Plant cells** contain all of the above PLUS:\n• Cell wall — made of cellulose, provides structural support (not all cells)\n• Permanent vacuole — filled with cell sap, maintains turgor pressure\n• Chloroplasts — contain chlorophyll, site of photosynthesis (only in cells exposed to light)\n\n**PROKARYOTIC CELLS** (bacteria) — NO true nucleus, much smaller (0.1–10 μm):\n• DNA — single circular loop free in cytoplasm (no membrane around it)\n• Plasmids — small circular DNA loops, often carry antibiotic resistance genes\n• Cell membrane — controls entry/exit of substances\n• Cell wall — made of peptidoglycan (different from plant cell walls)\n• Ribosomes — smaller than eukaryotic ribosomes (70S vs 80S)\n• No mitochondria, no chloroplasts\n\n**MICROSCOPY**\n• Light microscopes: max magnification ~×1500, resolution ~200 nm. Can view living specimens in colour.\n• Electron microscopes: max magnification ~×500,000, resolution ~0.2 nm. Can see internal organelles but specimens must be dead.\n• Magnification = Image size ÷ Actual size (rearrange with the formula triangle)\n• Units: 1 mm = 1000 μm = 1,000,000 nm\n\n**SPECIALISED CELLS** — cells differentiate to become specialised:\n• Sperm cell: tail for movement, many mitochondria, acrosome with enzymes, streamlined head\n• Nerve cell: long axon, myelin sheath for fast transmission, dendrites\n• Root hair cell: long extension for large surface area, many mitochondria for active transport\n• Muscle cell: many mitochondria, actin/myosin proteins for contraction',
